WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:08.000
"We're not gonna be little children anymore, we're gonna go into this as a job.

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:10.220
We're gonna take this seriously, and we're gonna make it to the top."

00:00:10.220 --> 00:00:25.220
(Casting)

00:00:25.220 --> 00:00:31.620
"At his prime he was definitely top 2. If not the best NA Midlaner, strictly because he was able to make these

00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:33.320
kind of calls that would win games."

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:38.280
"If they go to playoffs then choke it's on them, it's on us, it's on me."

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:51.740
(Casting)

00:00:55.020 --> 00:00:59.300
(Casting)

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:04.300
"I find it absolutely unbearable for us to take anything below Top 2."

00:01:10.580 --> 00:01:12.160
Back in 2009, a

00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:18.000
small indie game developer out of Santa Monica, California launched their inaugural game.

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:23.760
It was called League of Legends: Clash of Fates, and among the first 100 people to ever

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:29.120
play the game was a teenager by the name of Andy “Reginald” Dinh.

00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:30.460
And he didn’t like it.

00:01:30.460 --> 00:01:33.840
"At that point, I thought the game was pretty bad to be honest.

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:39.980
And so I actually quit the game because I came from Dota and there wasn’t lock-screen.

00:01:40.520 --> 00:01:44.960
I played League with lockscreen and I didn’t know I could unlock the screen, so I quit

00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:46.240
for a while and then I came back.”

00:01:46.260 --> 00:01:51.220
After that, Regi was hooked and while playing with his brother Dan, discovered that he had

00:01:51.220 --> 00:01:53.430
a natural talent for the game.

00:01:53.430 --> 00:01:57.430
Together, the Dinh brothers founded a League of Legends community forum and educational website called

00:01:57.430 --> 00:01:59.180
solomid.net.

00:01:59.180 --> 00:02:04.740
While, the duo ran their website, they were also competing on a team called All or Nothing.

00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:09.060
But because Regi and his brother were both trying to call the shots, Regi decided to

00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:11.980
briefly play for Counter Logic Gaming.

00:02:11.980 --> 00:02:17.000
Feeling he could do better on his own, Regi decided it was time to start a team.

00:02:17.240 --> 00:02:23.200
"TSM was formed because, I was on CLG and then I wanted a team for myself, so I was like

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:27.740
"Rainman join my team" and he was like "Oh I need a computer." I was like, "Yo I'll get you a computer."

00:02:27.740 --> 00:02:29.420
"So I liked bribed him to be my hoe."

00:02:29.420 --> 00:02:33.420
And I guess I got Chaox cause he's some Asian guy that wanted to be good at League of Legends."

00:02:33.560 --> 00:02:40.020
After going through a few early roster iterations, the TSM line-up settled on Reginald, TheOddOne,

00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:42.800
Chaox, TheRainMan and Xpecial.

00:02:42.800 --> 00:02:47.420
The newly formed TSM would attend the first League of Legends World Championship in Jönköping,

00:02:47.430 --> 00:02:52.370
Sweden, where they would test their mettle against the best North America, Southeast

00:02:52.370 --> 00:02:54.240
Asia and Europe had to offer.

00:02:54.320 --> 00:03:52.860
(Casting)

00:03:52.880 --> 00:03:54.230
Ultimately, TSM

00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:57.720
would place third at the event, losing to Against All Authority.

00:03:57.880 --> 00:04:06.840
(Casting)

00:04:06.920 --> 00:04:10.879
But that disappointment was short-lived when Riot announced Season 2 which would field

00:04:10.879 --> 00:04:16.459
a huge, international tournament circuit, with $5 million in prize pools and a chance

00:04:16.460 --> 00:04:19.540
to play at the Season 2 World Championship.

00:04:19.540 --> 00:04:24.860
Regi was thrilled and he wanted TSM to take the Season 2 circuit seriously, so he moved

00:04:24.860 --> 00:04:28.250
the team into a gaming house in Long Island, New York.

00:04:28.250 --> 00:04:34.270
“It was not a calculated business decision at all, I just decided to do it out of passion.

00:04:34.270 --> 00:04:39.379
I wanted to compete and that passion was more important to me than anything, money, or whatever

00:04:39.380 --> 00:04:40.000
it was.”

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:45.600
There, TSM would commit to a tougher scrim schedule and focus on League of Legends full-time.

00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:50.560
But the team’s top laner Christian “TheRainMan” Kahmann had different ideas.

00:04:50.560 --> 00:04:56.280
"For me as an individual player, I feel like just scrimming all day wasn't making me better as a player.

00:04:56.280 --> 00:05:02.560
so that's why I was feeling the way I was feeling. And I think my skill had gone on a general decline."

00:05:02.560 --> 00:05:07.040
"He didn't want to do our practice schedule and he said it was a waste of time. He did not feel like

00:05:07.040 --> 00:05:10.360
we would be able to win Season 2 if we stuck with our practice schedule."

00:05:10.360 --> 00:05:14.560
Regi and RainMan had been friends, but if he wasn’t willing to put in the work, Regi

00:05:14.580 --> 00:05:16.980
and TSM would move on without him.

00:05:16.980 --> 00:05:21.420
Eventually, Marcus “Dyrus” Hill, who was living in the house at the time, replaced

00:05:21.420 --> 00:05:23.020
TheRainMan on the team.

00:05:23.039 --> 00:05:28.009
For Regi, pushing this new work ethic and moving forward with Dyrus was just the first

00:05:28.009 --> 00:05:33.310
of many points where he’d have to put his position as team owner ahead of his personal

00:05:33.310 --> 00:05:34.310
relationships.

00:05:34.310 --> 00:05:37.560
And with Dyrus, they found success almost immediately.

00:05:37.580 --> 00:06:00.140
(Casting)

00:06:00.140 --> 00:06:01.040
As TSM

00:06:01.040 --> 00:06:05.820
scrimmed out of their Long Island gaming house, the rest of the nascent Western esports scene

00:06:05.820 --> 00:06:07.500
was developing around them.

00:06:07.520 --> 00:06:12.000
And Regi was beginning to cement himself as one of NA’s greatest mids.

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:30.640
(Casting)

00:06:30.640 --> 00:06:35.360
“He was was definitely Top 2 if not the best NA mid laner strictly because he was

00:06:35.360 --> 00:06:39.040
able to make these kind of calls that would win games”

00:06:39.040 --> 00:06:58.960
(Casting)

00:06:59.000 --> 00:07:01.600
“We were together and we played as a team."

00:07:01.600 --> 00:07:05.460
"And I attribute that to myself as I was able to pull everyone on the same page.

00:07:05.460 --> 00:07:08.340
five people doing something wrong is better than five

00:07:08.340 --> 00:07:12.900
guys doing five different things right” Driven by their fearless, shotcalling leader,

00:07:12.960 --> 00:07:20.640
TSM established themselves as North America’s premier LoL team.

00:07:20.720 --> 00:08:25.280
(Casting)

00:08:25.280 --> 00:08:31.000
TSM were riding high heading into the Season 2 World Championships, and as NA’s top seed,

00:08:31.009 --> 00:08:34.260
they guaranteed themselves a spot in the quarterfinals.

00:08:34.260 --> 00:08:35.260
Their opponents?

00:08:35.260 --> 00:08:37.380
Korea’s Azubu Frost.

00:08:37.380 --> 00:08:43.280
Frost dominated group A and entered the quarterfinal showdown with supreme confidence.

00:08:43.280 --> 00:08:48.400
And while TSM fought valiantly over the course of two games, in the end, Frost proved to

00:08:48.400 --> 00:08:50.280
be too much for them to handle.

00:08:50.320 --> 00:09:12.940
(Casting)

00:09:12.940 --> 00:09:15.320
But their defeat was not without controversy.

00:09:15.360 --> 00:09:20.280
"30 seconds into the game, when four of us are up at the tower, there was a pause.

00:09:20.280 --> 00:09:22.380
A very sudden pause by Azubu Frost.

00:09:22.740 --> 00:09:27.400
And ironically we were having problems with our chats, our chats weren’t working, our

00:09:27.400 --> 00:09:30.540
computers were slow [...] We did not care, we just wanted to play."

00:09:30.540 --> 00:09:34.260
And as soon as we saw that pause we were like
“Holy shit, our plan might not work.”

00:09:34.260 --> 00:09:40.960
We looked straight to Azubu Frost and Chaox notices their players looking at our map,

00:09:40.960 --> 00:09:43.640
looking at our mini-map and they started talking.

00:09:44.660 --> 00:09:49.820
There’s also proof in the replay that there’s been pings at dark areas where we’re standing

00:09:49.820 --> 00:09:51.240
where they have no vision.

00:09:51.300 --> 00:09:56.660
So that’s kind of shady, in my opinion."

00:09:56.700 --> 00:10:02.500
Despite a frustrating end to season 2, TSM's disappointment was short-lived as Riot announced

00:10:02.510 --> 00:10:06.600
that they would be moving to a league system in North America and Europe.

00:10:06.600 --> 00:10:12.570
And, as one of three NA teams to have attended the World Championship, TSM were guaranteed

00:10:12.570 --> 00:10:13.600
a spot in it.

00:10:13.600 --> 00:10:18.960
They entered the Spring Split as one of the strongest teams in NA and immediately looked the part

00:10:18.960 --> 00:11:24.020
(Casting)

00:11:24.020 --> 00:11:24.520
But 
while

00:11:24.520 --> 00:11:26.320
they looked strong throughout the first five weeks,

00:11:26.320 --> 00:11:33.240
an increasingly large disconnect began to form between AD carry Chaox and Regi, resulting

00:11:33.240 --> 00:11:37.580
in Jason "WildTurtle" Tran being brought in as the new
ADC.

00:11:37.580 --> 00:11:40.460
And his debut on the team was explosive.

00:11:40.460 --> 00:11:43.120
(Casting)

00:11:43.120 --> 00:12:04.660
(Casting)

00:12:04.660 --> 00:12:09.740
"Chaox you have something to answer right now because your replacement is doing a fantastic job."

00:12:09.740 --> 00:12:12.780
(Casting)

00:12:12.780 --> 00:12:18.680
Though Regi and Chaox were close friends, Regi wanted a very specific culture in TSM.

00:12:18.800 --> 00:12:22.040
One that prioritized hard work above everything else.

00:12:22.040 --> 00:12:26.880
“You don’t show up to work late constantly and expect to not get punished”

00:12:32.920 --> 00:12:38.740
“We have to set a precedent because if someone gets away with doing something that bad, it’s

00:12:38.740 --> 00:12:40.000
not good for anything.

00:12:40.000 --> 00:12:41.040
It’s just toxic.”

00:12:41.040 --> 00:12:46.480
Once again, Regi had to choose business over friendship, resulting in the team moving forward

00:12:46.480 --> 00:12:48.600
with Turtle instead of Chaox.

00:12:48.600 --> 00:12:54.460
While Turtle may not have been miles ahead of the other ADCs in NA at the time, his attitude

00:12:54.460 --> 00:12:57.040
better matched Regi’s vision for TSM.

00:12:57.040 --> 00:13:01.160
"He wasn't that much better than Chaox, but because he took the game a lot more seriously at that point,

00:13:01.160 --> 00:13:03.500
he had a much higher ceiling and we were super excited about that."

00:13:03.720 --> 00:13:09.240
but the reason why TSM got a lot better was by getting rid of Chaox because he wasn’t serious,

00:13:09.260 --> 00:13:11.780
it made everyone on our team be more serious.”

00:13:11.780 --> 00:13:16.640
With a boost from Turtle, TSM went on to win the first NA LCS split.

00:13:16.640 --> 00:13:33.360
(Casting)

00:13:35.000 --> 00:13:39.460
But in the summer, a new obstacle emerged in the form of Cloud9.

00:13:39.460 --> 00:13:44.080
A plucky team of newcomers who had qualified through the first split of North American

00:13:44.080 --> 00:13:46.420
Challenger as Quantic Gaming.

00:13:46.420 --> 00:13:51.660
TSM were still a strong team that summer, but Cloud9 were a force, and when it came

00:13:51.680 --> 00:13:56.640
time for the playoffs, Regi and the rest of TSM were simply no match.

00:13:56.720 --> 00:14:16.320
(Casting)

00:14:16.320 --> 00:14:20.800
One disappointment would lead to another, as TSM bombed out of groups in the Season

00:14:20.800 --> 00:14:22.500
3 World Championship.

00:14:22.500 --> 00:14:27.640
One of Regi’s lane opponents at Worlds was a young mid lane prodigy who was playing outside

00:14:27.640 --> 00:14:29.310
of Korea for the first time.

00:14:29.320 --> 00:14:35.360
Even then, it was obvious that against a player like Faker, Regi was out of his depth.

00:14:35.400 --> 00:14:44.200
(Casting)

00:14:44.200 --> 00:14:49.870
“...his mechanical ceiling was so much higher than mine that I felt I was put in a position

00:14:49.870 --> 00:14:53.080
where I had to play to not lose and go even.

00:14:53.440 --> 00:14:57.720
It was one of the most uncomfortable playing experiences I’ve had in my career.”

00:14:57.720 --> 00:15:00.580
Reginald found himself in a difficult position.

00:15:00.580 --> 00:15:02.740
He was a veteran of the professional scene.

00:15:02.750 --> 00:15:06.420
A pioneer that had helped pave the way for the Western esports boom.

00:15:06.420 --> 00:15:13.320
But he was also a mid laner on a team that would only face stiffer competition heading into 2014.

00:15:13.320 --> 00:15:17.000
A mid laner who, unlike his rivals, owned his team.

00:15:17.000 --> 00:15:21.140
Because of the responsibilities that came with being a team owner, Regi couldn’t play

00:15:21.140 --> 00:15:22.589
as much as his players.

00:15:22.589 --> 00:15:26.920
He couldn’t throw himself into solo queue after scrims, he couldn’t fully dedicate

00:15:26.920 --> 00:15:28.600
himself to the game.

00:15:28.600 --> 00:15:30.520
And he needed someone who could.

00:15:36.800 --> 00:15:37.580
“I’m Bjergsen.

00:15:37.580 --> 00:15:39.100
I heard you guys need a new mid laner.”

00:15:39.380 --> 00:15:41.340
“Who the fuck are you?”

00:15:41.340 --> 00:15:46.620
Soren “Bjergsen” Bjerg had been making a name for himself in the European LCS as

00:15:46.640 --> 00:15:50.480
a bright star on a struggling Copenhagen Wolves roster.

00:15:50.520 --> 00:16:28.040
(Casting)

00:16:28.040 --> 00:16:32.540
Despite Bjergsen’s potential, the decision to go with him was a difficult one,

00:16:32.560 --> 00:16:40.480
especially because another, far more experienced mid laner was in the mix — Enrique "xPeke" Cedeño Martínez.

00:16:40.480 --> 00:16:45.760
Regi was his team's primary shotcaller and he needed someone who could fill those shoes.

00:16:45.760 --> 00:16:50.820
So the choice to go with a young, relatively unproven mechanical genius over a proven,

00:16:50.820 --> 00:16:53.480
legend in xPeke was a risky one.

00:16:53.480 --> 00:16:57.980
Initially Bjergsen struggled as the team’s in-game leader, but it was something Regi

00:16:58.000 --> 00:16:59.840
would help him learn over time.

00:16:59.840 --> 00:17:02.000
"Kind of just saying well it's their fault because four of them are there."

00:17:02.000 --> 00:17:06.200
"Then basically what you're doing you're not even shotcalling you're just basically asking people questions"

00:17:06.620 --> 00:17:07.580
"Like should we fight or not?"

00:17:07.580 --> 00:17:10.220
"Like I never wanted to be the shotcaller it was put on me."

00:17:12.000 --> 00:17:13.600
"That's an overall shotcall."

00:17:13.880 --> 00:17:17.320
"That's the shotcaller's point they get all the information they need and then make a final decision."

00:17:17.320 --> 00:17:22.340
"My problem there is no one made a call. People should be actively feeding you information.

00:17:22.340 --> 00:17:23.980
and then you should make a final call."

00:17:23.980 --> 00:17:30.100
After falling again to Cloud9 in the Spring 2014 finals, Regi made further roster changes,

00:17:30.100 --> 00:17:32.140
bringing in Amazing and Lustboy.

00:17:32.140 --> 00:17:36.530
And with their new jungler and support, TSM finally wrenched back the title from their

00:17:36.530 --> 00:17:38.160
rivals in the Summer.

00:17:38.220 --> 00:18:01.240
(Casting)

00:18:01.240 --> 00:18:07.400
The team that had first stormed across the NA scene was almost unrecognizable, with Dyrus being the

00:18:07.400 --> 00:18:09.880
last old-guard member of the roster.

00:18:09.880 --> 00:18:14.420
But despite the new players, TSM still played, largely, like TSM.

00:18:14.420 --> 00:18:18.580
They focused around a strong, decisive mid laner and they used support from the jungle

00:18:18.580 --> 00:18:21.280
to take pressure off their other lanes.

00:18:21.280 --> 00:18:46.880
(Casting)

00:18:46.960 --> 00:18:53.120
At Worlds 2014, TSM would have to once again fight for their spot in the Top 8.

00:18:53.120 --> 00:18:57.360
But after making it through the group stage, they ran into one of the tournament favorites

00:18:57.360 --> 00:18:58.620
in Samsung White.

00:18:58.620 --> 00:19:02.820
"Undefeated records, KDA leaders in four out of the five positions on this team."

00:19:02.880 --> 00:19:07.820
TSM were clearly outmatched, and though they managed to get a win off the back off some

00:19:07.840 --> 00:19:12.320
questionable drafting by White, they found themselves overwhelmed.

00:19:12.320 --> 00:19:34.720
(Casting)

00:19:34.780 --> 00:19:41.380
Although 2014 ended in international disappointment for TSM, Regi was undergoing evolution as

00:19:41.380 --> 00:19:42.300
a team owner.

00:19:42.300 --> 00:19:48.120
2015 saw the TSM brand expand into a number of games outside of League of Legends.

00:19:48.120 --> 00:19:53.880
In January, TSM acquired the Danish CS:GO team that would go on to become Astralis.

00:19:53.880 --> 00:19:59.720
Featuring dupreeh, Xyp9x, device, cajunb and karrigan, the roster was a dominant force

00:19:59.720 --> 00:20:06.160
throughout much of the 2015 season, cementing themselves as one of the best teams in the world.

00:20:06.300 --> 00:20:11.860
It was Regi’s first big statement that TSM was interested in being more than just a League

00:20:11.860 --> 00:20:13.010
of Legends team.

00:20:13.010 --> 00:20:17.570
But Regi wasn’t satisfied with only dipping his toes into CS:GO.

00:20:17.570 --> 00:20:23.910
TSM would go on to pick-up players and rosters in Super Smash Bros, Call of Duty, Smite,

00:20:23.910 --> 00:20:27.320
Vainglory, Hearthstone and more recently,
PUBG and Fortnite.

00:20:27.320 --> 00:20:33.500
But as TSM the organization was expanding, its League of Legends team, had begun to falter.

00:20:33.510 --> 00:20:39.490
The team had suffered back-to-back defeats in the LCS at the hands of CLG in Summer 2015

00:20:39.490 --> 00:20:44.140
and Spring 2016 After a disappointing early exit from Worlds

00:20:44.140 --> 00:20:51.210
2015’s group stage, followed by Dyrus’ tearful departure at the end of that event, it was clear that something

00:20:51.210 --> 00:20:52.640
needed to change.

00:20:54.740 --> 00:20:59.940
The next year, would be a transitional one for TSM, as Reginald attempted to reinvent

00:20:59.940 --> 00:21:01.720
the championship winning formula.

00:21:01.720 --> 00:21:05.380
And by the summer of 2016, he’d done just that.

00:21:05.380 --> 00:21:10.640
Bjergsen, Doublelift, Hauntzer, Svenskeren, and Biofrost formed one of the most domestically

00:21:10.650 --> 00:21:13.540
dominant teams NA has ever seen.

00:21:13.540 --> 00:21:18.830
They adopted an aggressive practice schedule designed to mirror Korean teams, squeezing

00:21:18.830 --> 00:21:20.920
potential out of their new lineup.

00:21:20.920 --> 00:21:22.740
And in NA, it worked.

00:21:22.740 --> 00:23:05.000
(Casting)

00:23:05.980 --> 00:23:10.480
But TSM’s reign wouldn’t extend beyond their own region, and the team fell short

00:23:10.560 --> 00:23:12.420
on the international stage again,

00:23:12.420 --> 00:23:20.360
(Casting)

00:23:20.360 --> 00:23:21.240
and again,

00:23:21.320 --> 00:23:33.900
(Casting)

00:23:33.960 --> 00:23:34.680
and again.

00:23:34.720 --> 00:23:48.740
(Casting)

00:23:48.740 --> 00:23:53.800
And throughout this testing period, Regi had to learn another tough lesson — how to take

00:23:53.800 --> 00:23:58.140
a step back from the team he’d poured his heart and soul into.

00:23:58.140 --> 00:24:03.200
Throughout TSM’s history, Regi had built up a reputation for inserting himself into

00:24:03.200 --> 00:24:05.060
the team when things got tough.

00:24:05.060 --> 00:24:10.520
Most notably in 2015, when he took over as head coach — enforcing his philosophies

00:24:10.520 --> 00:24:12.240
about how a team should be run.

00:24:12.240 --> 00:24:15.920
“Although you know, the community and everyone blames the ‘Hey you know Reginald only steps

00:24:15.920 --> 00:24:20.360
in because he wants to get all the spotlight around his team,’ that’s actually not the case.

00:24:20.360 --> 00:24:28.040
I’m stepping in because I find it absolutely unbearable for us to take anything below Top 2.”

00:24:28.040 --> 00:24:31.760
In most of these instances, Regi got the result he wanted.

00:24:31.760 --> 00:24:36.580
But, over time, it became clear that he needed to have faith in the team and staff that he’d

00:24:36.580 --> 00:24:39.840
built, in the empire he’d helped to create.

00:24:39.840 --> 00:24:44.440
When Regi stepped in, it could hurt his coach’s authority, and coaching infrastructure was

00:24:44.440 --> 00:24:46.700
becoming a bigger part of the LCS.

00:24:46.700 --> 00:24:48.760
And it’s a lesson he appeared to learn.

00:24:48.760 --> 00:24:54.480
In the 2018 Spring Split, TSM struggled to adapt to a new roster, and eventually was

00:24:54.480 --> 00:24:58.880
eliminated in the playoff quarterfinals after a tough regular season.

00:24:58.880 --> 00:25:32.180
(Casting)

00:25:32.180 --> 00:25:37.120
Regi, though supportive of his players and staff, let the split play itself out.

00:25:39.140 --> 00:25:43.840
Regi’s maturation from a teenaged League of Legends enthusiast to the owner of one

00:25:43.860 --> 00:25:47.700
of the biggest esports brands in the world had given him a platform.

00:25:47.700 --> 00:25:53.000
And during an interview in 2016, he used that platform to highlight the risks of running

00:25:53.010 --> 00:25:58.110
a League of Legends franchise and how the majority of the teams were not making money

00:25:58.120 --> 00:25:59.720
from their LCS ventures.

00:25:59.800 --> 00:26:05.920
"It's just an unstable environment overall and I would say it's really volatile for the organizations and the players.

00:26:05.920 --> 00:26:12.040
And like if you want to be a news anchor you put time into it you slowly improve it over the years and by

00:26:12.040 --> 00:26:15.120
the time you're in it for 20 years you become an absolute star

00:26:15.120 --> 00:26:17.020
and you're not nervous in front of a camera anymore.

00:26:17.020 --> 00:26:22.980
I kind of want that to be like my career in esports where I'm investing into something stable."

00:26:23.180 --> 00:26:26.860
"And I think it's just a combination of the lack of revenue and also

00:26:26.880 --> 00:26:32.400
how unstable the patches are that really makes things volatile in terms of the investment perspective.”

00:26:32.400 --> 00:26:36.740
After Regi’s interview, Riot co-founder Marc “Tryndamere” Merill took to reddit

00:26:36.740 --> 00:26:42.860
with a now infamous response that entirely missed the point that Regi had been attempting to make.

00:26:43.080 --> 00:26:47.640
The resulting backlash towards Merill and Riot from the community led to a letter signed

00:26:47.640 --> 00:26:51.390
by most LCS team owners, asking for a new system.

00:26:51.390 --> 00:26:57.460
And, by June 2017, Riot announced a new, franchised system in North America.

00:26:57.460 --> 00:27:03.520
With multi-million dollar buy-ins, infrastructure and revenue sharing, the new NA LCS has opened

00:27:03.520 --> 00:27:07.210
a debate about what a modern esports league should look like.

00:27:07.210 --> 00:27:10.660
It’s a big step for the scene, and a big step for esports.

00:27:10.660 --> 00:27:14.340
Although the conversation about franchising was no doubt happening for some time, behind

00:27:14.340 --> 00:27:19.660
the scenes for some time, the public push on its behalf came in part because of Regi.

00:27:19.669 --> 00:27:25.110
"There is kind of a difference in public perception vs the real Regi.

00:27:25.110 --> 00:27:32.130
I’ve seen him go out of his way to take care of players and also set the industry

00:27:32.130 --> 00:27:33.690
standard for player treatment.”

00:27:33.690 --> 00:27:38.850
From the kid who just wanted to create a community around League of Legends, to one of the most

00:27:38.850 --> 00:27:44.230
influential owners in a new, franchised era, Regi has come a long way.

00:27:44.230 --> 00:27:48.500
There’s no question that Reginald was in the right place, at the right time.

00:27:48.500 --> 00:27:50.400
That he got to know the right people.

00:27:50.400 --> 00:27:55.620
But there’s also no question that he worked harder, that he hustled more, and that he

00:27:55.620 --> 00:27:58.950
helped to build the Western esports scene around him.

00:27:58.950 --> 00:28:01.420
That’s the thing about Regi, and about TSM.

00:28:01.420 --> 00:28:03.180
You can love them, or not.

00:28:03.180 --> 00:28:04.980
You can cheer for them, or not.

00:28:04.980 --> 00:28:08.080
But in esports, results are ultimately what matter.

00:28:08.080 --> 00:28:10.940
And you can’t argue with their results.

00:28:16.200 --> 00:28:20.980
Thanks for watching. If you want more great content just like this, be sure to hit the subscribe button.

